.TH DQL-QUERY 1 "2026-07-05" "dql 0.1.0" "DelightQL Manual"
.SH NAME
dql-query \- compile and execute a DelightQL query
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dql query
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
[\fIQUERY\fR]
.br
.B dql q
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
[\fIQUERY\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Compiles a DelightQL query to SQL and, by default, executes it and
prints the results. The query source is chosen in this order:
.IP \(bu 3
the
.I QUERY
argument, if present;
.IP \(bu 3
the file named by
.BR \-\-file ;
.IP \(bu 3
standard input, when stdin is not a terminal;
.IP \(bu 3
otherwise an interactive REPL is started.
.PP
A file (or stdin) may contain multiple statements; all of them are
executed and their output concatenated.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Input
.TP
.BI \-\-file " FILE"
Read the query from
.IR FILE ;
a
.I FILE
of
.B \-
means stdin.
.TP
.B \-\-sequential
Execute multiple queries sequentially (for files with multiple
queries).
.TP
.BI \-\-consult " FILE"
Unsupported \(em refuses with a pointer: use
.B consult!()
in the DQL source instead, which also works in files, test balls, and
the REPL.
.TP
.BI \-\-attach " ATTACH"
Attach an external database.
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-interactive
Interactive mode \(em allow dot commands.
.SS Compilation
.TP
.BI \-\-to " STAGE"
Stop at an intermediate stage and print its representation instead of
executing. Stages, in pipeline order:
.BR cst ,
.BR ast\-unresolved ,
.BR ast\-resolved ,
.BR ast\-refined ,
.BR ast\-sql ,
.BR sql ,
.B results
(default),
.BR fingerprint ,
.BR hash ,
.BR bhash ,
.BR totalhash .
.RB ( recursion\-depth
is deprecated; use sys::execution.stack(*) instead.)
.IP
.B hash
prints a column-name-independent data hash;
.B totalhash
includes column names;
.B bhash
is the byte-level, type-preserving, platform-independent hash;
.B fingerprint
prints result-fingerprint JSON for semantic comparison.
.IP
A query that fails to compile under
.B \-\-to
emits the same structured error record as normal execution \(em URI
and full message \(em and exits 1; the inspection surface never knows
less than the execution surface about why a compile failed. (Under
the hood both are projections of
.IR sys::execution.compile ,
whose relation carries
.I error
= the URI and
.I error_message
= the prose.)
.IP
Note that stage output is itself delivered as a one-column relation
named
.IR representation ,
so
.B \-\-to sql
in the default format prints a header line and the SQL with newlines
escaped as \en. Use
.B \-f list
for readable multi-line SQL, or
.B \-n
to drop the header.
.TP
.BI \-\-dialect " DIALECT"
Target dialect for the generated SQL. See
.BR dql (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-soptimize " N"
SQL optimization level, 0\-3. Default 0.
.SS Output
.TP
.BI \-f ", " \-\-format " FORMAT"
Output format. One of
.BR table ,
.BR box ,
.BR json ,
.BR jsonl ,
.BR csv ,
.BR tsv ,
.BR list ,
.BR raw .
The default is tab-separated values with a header row.
.B json
emits one array;
.B jsonl
emits one object per row (streams; composes with jq). Both are typed:
a column DECLARED numeric whose text round-trips emits an unquoted
number, SQL NULL emits
.BR null ,
everything else is a string \(em a TEXT '007' stays "007", and
undeclared columns (expressions, CTE results) stay strings. Keys keep
the relation's column order.
.B table
and
.B box
draw an aligned pipe-separated grid.
.B list
prints one
.I column = value
line per value with a blank line between rows.
.B raw
is byte-faithful extraction of exactly ONE column: cell bytes are
written verbatim with no separators (a separator would corrupt binary
data), NULL writes zero bytes, and rows concatenate as a byte stream.
Multi-column results are refused. Writing raw bytes to a terminal
prints a warning on stderr (control sequences pass through verbatim);
raw is intended for pipes and files \(em it is the one output path
that never converts bytes to text. With
.BR \-\-to " sql"
it instead prints the bare compile artifact with a trailing newline.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-no\-headers
Suppress the header row.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Suppress the REPL banner and meta output. Only meaningful in
interactive mode; no effect on query output paths.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sanitize
Disable output sanitization (allows raw terminal control sequences
through).
.TP
.BI \-\-highlights " FILE"
Path to a highlights.scm file.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " FILE"
Path to a theme file.
.SS Database
.TP
.BI \-\-db " DATABASE"
SQLite database file. Optional; default is an in-memory database.
.TP
.B \-\-make\-new\-db\-if\-missing
Create the
.B \-\-db
file if it does not exist. Without this flag a missing file is a
refusal, not a creation. Global, like
.B \-\-db
itself \(em it also works with bare
.B dql
to start the REPL on a fresh database (see
.BR dql (1)).
.SS Instrumentation and gates
.TP
.BI \-\-debug " OPTS"
Debug options, comma-separated.
.TP
.BI \-\-sink " NAME=PATH"
Bind a named emit stream to a file sink. Unbound streams fall back to
RS-prefixed records on stderr. May be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-\-danger " HIERARCHY=STATE"
Open a danger gate for this session. STATE is ON, OFF, ALLOW, or 1\-9.
Example:
.BR "\-\-danger cardinality/nulljoin=ON" .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " HIERARCHY=STATE"
Set a config for this session. Same STATE grammar as
.BR \-\-danger .
.TP
.BI \-\-error\-prefix " PREFIX"
See
.BR dql (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Query compiled and executed.
.TP
.B 1
Compile or runtime error; one structured record per error on stderr:
.IP
.EX
<RS>[delightql-error://semantic/resolution/table] Table not found: nope
.EE
.IP
Feed the bracketed identifier to
.BR dql-explain (1)
for the full story.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
$ dql query --db app.db 'users(*), age > 30 |> (id, first_name)'
$ echo 'users(*) ~> count:(*)' | dql query --db app.db
$ dql query --db app.db --to sql -f list 'users(*), age > 30'
$ dql query --db app.db --to bhash 'orders(*)'   # regression pin
$ dql query -f json '_(name, age @ "Ada", 36)'   # no db needed
$ dql query --db app.db --to sql -f raw 'users(*)'  # bare pasteable SQL
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dql (1),
.BR dql-explain (1),
.BR dql-format (1)
